<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Friedrich Böttger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1682–1719</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Friedrich Böttger was a German alchemist held at the court of Augustus the Strong, who expected him to make gold. Working with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ehrenfried-walther-von-tschirnhaus">Ehrenfried Walther von Tschirnhaus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, he produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s first true hard-paste <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> around 1708–1709 and became the first administrator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> porcelain manufactory founded in 1710. He also developed a fine red stoneware known as Böttger ware.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-friedrich-bottger.jpg" alt="Johann Friedrich Böttger" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eduard Sommer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saxonia_Museum_f%C3%BCr_saechsische_Vaterlandskunde_I_33.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
